      "raw": "<p>Hey, I came to this forum for the first time ever just to learn more about the rules of the rpg, and I have a couple questions for the devs regarding mods.</p><p>1: The answers the mods have given to other mod-related questions seems to imply that, A) when a mod is applied is decided right before it&#39;s applied and not when it&#39;s created, and B) the creator of the mod is always the one who makes that decision, and that doesn&#39;t sound right to me. Say Hero A decides to boost* Hero B with a &quot;+2 pipe wrench&quot;, and later Hero B wants to swing that wrench at an enemy. Even though Hero B is the doing the swinging, Hero A is the one who made the bonus, so they&#39;re the one who decides whether the bonus is applied or not, and that doesn&#39;t seem very fair to Hero B. I must be missing something, and can use some clarification.</p><p>2: The handbook says you can&#39;t &quot;make change&quot; with opposing mods, but if you&#39;re, say, boosting* a target that has a penalty on it, does that mean you <em>have</em> to roll to remove the penalty, or can you ignore it and add a bonus on top of that as well? If the latter, why would you ever choose to remove a mod when adding a mod seems to always be the more efficient option? On a related note, since persistant mods are meant to last until the end of the scene, is boosting/hindering even able to remove those at all?</p><p>*I&#39;m using boosts in the examples, but both questions apply to hinders as well, so of course I want to know if the answer is at all different between boosts and hinders.</p>",
